{
  "data": {
    "text": "Longitudinal Changes in the Cerebral Cortex Functional Organization of Healthy Elderly. Joanna Su Xian Chong et al. The Journal of Neuroscience, 2019. https://doi.org/10.1523/JNEUROSCI.1451-18.2019. Healthy aging is accompanied by disruptions in the functional modular organization of the human brain.\n\nSeventy-two healthy elderly Chinese adults from the Singapore-Longitudinal Aging Brain Study were included in the longitudinal analyses. Age (years) 68.22 (5.80), range = 58.64–84.91. Sex (female/male) 33/39. Handedness (right/left) 68/4. Participants met the following criteria at all time points: (1) scored ≥ 24 in the Mini-Mental State Examination; (2) scored ≤ 9 in the modified Geriatric Depression Screening Scale; and (3) did not have the following conditions: history of significant vascular events (myocardial infarction, stroke, or peripheral vascular disease), history of cardiac, lung, liver, or kidney failure, history of malignant neoplasia of any form, history of head trauma with loss of consciousness, active or inadequately treated thyroid disease, or active neurological or psychiatric conditions. The study was approved by the Institutional Review Board of the National University of Singapore, and written informed consent was obtained from all participants.\n\nFor the cross-sectional analyses, we examined baseline neuroimaging data from a separate dataset comprising 57 healthy young Chinese adults (mean ± SD age, 23.71 ± 3.61 years; range 18.59–34.08 years; 22 males; 55 right-handed). All healthy young participants fulfilled the following criteria: no history of psychiatric or neurologic disorders, no history of severe medical illnesses, not taking any long-term psychoactive medication, nonsmoker, and consuming ≤ 1 unit of alcohol per week.\n\nBoth healthy young and elderly participants were scanned at the Centre for Cognitive Neuroscience, Duke-NUS Medical School, on a 3T Siemens Magnetom Tim Trio System. The scan protocol included a high-resolution T1-weighted structural scan and an 8 min T2*-weighted task-free functional MRI scan where participants were instructed to fixate on a cross in the center of the screen (echo planar sequence, repetition time = 2000 ms, echo time = 30 ms, flip angle = 90°, field of view = 192 × 192 mm2, matrix size = 64 × 64, 36 continuous axial slices, voxel size = 3.0 × 3.0 × 3.0 mm3).\n\nFunctional imaging. fMRI images were preprocessed using a previous protocol based on the Analysis of Functional NeuroImages software and the FMRIB Software Library. Preprocessing steps included the following: (1) removal of first 5 volumes for magnetic field stabilization; (2) slice-time and motion correction; (3) time series despiking and grand mean scaling; (4) spatial smoothing using a 6 mm FWHM Gaussian kernel; (5) bandpass temporal filtering (0.009–0.1 Hz); (6) removal of linear and quadratic trends; (7) structural image coregistration using boundary-based registration, followed by nonlinear registration (FNIRT) of functional images to standard MNI152 space; (8) regression of nuisance signals (white matter, CSF, global signal, and six motion parameters); and (9) motion scrubbing of volumes with framewise displacement ≥ 0.2 mm and DVARS ≥ 0.5%, with 1 volume before and 2 volumes after also removed.\n\nUndirected, weighted functional connectivity matrices were generated for each participant at each time point. Individual preprocessed functional images in standard space were first parcellated into 114 cortical regions of interest defined by a functional connectivity-based parcellation scheme. Functional graphs for each individual were then computed by calculating the correlation between the mean BOLD time series of each pair of regions. All diagonal elements and negative connections in the matrices were set to 0. Graph theoretical measures such as global efficiency, local efficiency, participation coefficient, within-module degree, and system segregation were computed using the Brain Connectivity Toolbox.\n\nNeuropsychological assessments comprised five domains: processing speed, attention, verbal memory, visuospatial memory, and executive functioning. For each cognitive domain, test scores at each time point were standardized to T scores (mean 50, SD 10) with respect to the baseline, and a composite score for each participant at each time point was obtained by averaging the standardized T scores across tests within that domain. A measure of global cognitive performance for each participant at each time point was derived by averaging across the T scores of the five cognitive domains."
  },
